WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.975 --> 00:00:04.620
- Well, this is a wild question from Marc

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:07.200
because I would argue

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:11.528
that my entire life has been that.

00:00:11.528 --> 00:00:13.570
(upbeat music)

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:15.181
- [Gary] You got more perspective.

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:16.261
(engine roaring)

00:00:16.261 --> 00:00:18.571
(crowd cheering)

00:00:18.571 --> 00:00:19.404
I just want to be happy.

00:00:19.404 --> 00:00:20.434
Don't you want to be happy?

00:00:20.434 --> 00:00:23.710
(upbeat music)

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:25.440
- This is gonna be kinda off the rails

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.920
to my opportunity to speak to Gary,

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:29.640
I'm gonna ask things that
I'm personally curious about.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.920
So I kinda have this theory

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:34.620
that kinda life has these phases to it.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:39.260
And for me kinda phase
one was up until I was 30

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:42.850
where I really had no
clue what I was about.

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:44.810
I didn't know what my passion was.

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:46.940
I didn't know really where I was going.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:48.300
And I was really lucky.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.630
I had this kind of mentor
who kinda grabbed me

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:52.877
and said, "How old are you?"

00:00:52.877 --> 00:00:54.410
And I said, "Well, I was 29."

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:55.740
And he goes, "Oh, okay."

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:57.910
He goes, "You don't have far to go."

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:00.040
He goes, "Usually when you hit 30,

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:03.270
all of a sudden everything becomes clear."

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:05.940
And got low and behold, he was right.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.950
Then all of a sudden I could
go, "Oh, I like what I'm doing.

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:10.930
I can see a path from here.

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:13.360
I have family now, I have balance."

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.340
And it set up this
wonderful period of my life.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:18.640
So here's what I was gonna bring up

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:23.013
is that the second period for
me started when I turned 45.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.030
The third period really throw it to 45.

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:31.840
And 45 was the year that I left Netflix.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:36.450
45 was a little bit after the Netflix IPO.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:39.410
And it was this real boundary for me

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:41.390
because I so distinctly remember

00:01:43.077 --> 00:01:45.210
I went to the IPO, we're in New York,

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:47.400
we were up in the Merrill
Lynch trading floor

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.180
and it was kinda anticlimactic.

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:50.670
You know, there was no bells to ring,

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:53.280
no confetti, just kinda
the trading started,

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.210
but it was a significant milestone.

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:56.600
And I remember leaving the building

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:59.190
and I was with my
eight-year-old son, Logan.

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:02.450
And we had a New York objective

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:05.727
'cause he grew up on California
as this bronze to me.

00:02:05.727 --> 00:02:08.630
And I go, "I'm gonna take him to get

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:10.710
some real New York pizza."

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:12.350
So we're in the cab and we're heading down

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:16.120
from the Merrill building
downtown to go to original Resy

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:17.700
like 80 of them now.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.927
But I remember thinking,

00:02:19.927 --> 00:02:22.430
"I could kinda do whatever I want now."

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:24.900
And it was a really weird feeling

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:26.820
'cause you think you have
all these opportunities,

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:30.530
but instead, it's kind of
throws you off balance.

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:34.630
So since in so many ways,

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:36.840
you and I have had these
similar trajectories

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:38.850
and I kinda know, we see
the world the same way

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.290
and we have these same
motivations in some ways,

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:43.350
some of the same superpower.

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:46.210
And I know that you just hit 45.

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:47.400
- Yes.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:48.480
- And I'm wondering,

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.523
do you feel that things
are changing for you?

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:54.100
- I do.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:55.930
And I think things are always changing.

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:56.763
And to your point,

00:02:56.763 --> 00:02:59.760
I think round numbers
get people thoughtful.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:01.670
I think people think differently

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:03.810
around their zero and five birthday

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.153
whether it's 20 or 25, 30, 35, right?

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:10.930
For me,

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:15.930
I, as a child spent a lot of time

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:18.447
hanging out with grandparents.

00:03:18.447 --> 00:03:20.797
And I've been thinking
about this a lot lately.

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:27.690
I was very gravitated towards,
you know, 70, 80 year olds

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:31.063
when I was 7, 8, 9, 10.

00:03:31.989 --> 00:03:32.822
It was just a thing.

00:03:32.822 --> 00:03:35.630
I was very close to my great-grandfather

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:37.820
when we first immigrated to America.

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:39.400
Spent a lot of time with him

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:42.280
at the park when I was three, four,

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:43.320
but I remember it.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:44.480
I literally remember it more.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.810
Maybe it was more for four.

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:48.100
Then we moved to Jersey.

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:49.117
I remember like...

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:53.870
And then definitely
from like 1982 to 1988,

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:57.580
every time a grandparent
would come and visit,

00:03:57.580 --> 00:03:59.860
I just always felt this weird association

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:02.660
towards somebody would always
ask them stories, history.

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:07.100
And so I've always felt
in a lot of ways that

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:08.400
in hindsight as I got older,

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.310
I was like, "Oh, I'm an old soul.

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:11.220
I like wisdom."

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:12.840
Like, "Oh, I was such a bad student,

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:15.420
but history was something
I was always really..."

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:17.980
The only Bs I had on my report
card on regular classes,

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:20.823
not named Jim we're in history.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:24.340
And even like my current passion

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:28.590
in discovering what I think
is about to play out with NFTs

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:30.940
and the whole non-fungible
token blockchain.

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:34.040
A lot of it has to do with
psychology and history

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:35.343
and IP and all this stuff.

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:42.223
Things are changing, but not
from a wisdom standpoint.

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:44.283
You know?

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:48.410
I'll give you something tangible.

00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:51.860
I've come to realize recently
called last two, three years

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:54.780
that my greatest
vulnerability as a leader,

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:55.613
believe it or not,

00:04:55.613 --> 00:04:56.860
because people are confused by this

00:04:56.860 --> 00:05:00.510
'cause I'm so candorous on
interviews and on-stage.

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:05.510
But as an operator, I so struggle
with delivering bad news.

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:09.920
Believe it or not that
I've struggled with candor.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:12.560
And I've created this term
for myself, kind candor.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:15.340
I've come to realize
that it's a vulnerability

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:16.400
and I wanna be better.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:18.110
And all of us evolve and
we want to be better,

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:21.150
and kind candor is something

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:22.950
that I've stood up
recently in my own mind.

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:25.210
And now even within VaynerX,

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:26.043
my holding company.

00:05:26.043 --> 00:05:27.400
I want people to be able to do that

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:28.700
because it leads to entitlement

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:31.393
and resentment if you're
not deploying candor.

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:35.890
But I think what's happening for me is

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:37.540
I've always in my mind

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:43.170
put a premium on 50 to 60 years old.

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:46.580
I've always, probably
since I was about 19 or 20

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:47.980
started having these feelings

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:50.383
that 50 to 60 was gonna be the money shot.

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:53.540
And so, you know, when you're 26

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:55.307
and thinking about 50 to 60,

00:05:55.307 --> 00:05:57.840
you're like, "Oh, my God.
I have a whole life to live

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:01.320
before I even enter my
quote-unquote "Prime".

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:02.930
But now I'm 45,

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:05.690
and I'm like, "Okay, I'm
about to go into this thing

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:07.610
that I've put on a pedestal.

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:10.040
A thing that's been intuitive to me

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:11.040
and I'm excited about it."

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:12.800
I feel like I'm gearing up

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:16.530
and I feel experienced to
go along with the raw talent

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:18.320
that I was gifted with.

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:22.420
And, you know, I've
never lacked confidence

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:24.550
not because I was audacious,

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:27.490
but because my confidence
came from my conviction

00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:31.110
of appreciating the
process over the results.

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:32.330
- Yeah, the plunging in.

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:36.119
Just the "Let's do something
and let's see what happens."

00:06:36.119 --> 00:06:37.460
- Mm-hmm.

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:42.137
And so I'm pretty excited about this...

00:06:42.137 --> 00:06:44.287
- Do you still believe
it's the money shot?

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:50.130
- Yeah, you know, I do feel
like it makes sense to me.

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:52.250
And maybe when I'm 60, I'm
like, "No, wait a minute,

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:53.650
it was 70 that... you know."

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:59.843
But I do think that there's a
time and a place and a prime.

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:05.020
I think I've always felt that
business had more similarities

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:06.893
to sport than people realized.

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:11.760
There's been no athlete that's come along

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:13.370
and has been consistently the same athlete

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:17.100
for all 8, 12, 15 years
of her or his career.

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:19.662
There is a prime and...

00:07:19.662 --> 00:07:21.660
- Oh, yeah, I get it.

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:23.150
The thing that I'm trying to curious

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:25.220
and I'll check in with you in five years

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:28.130
and see how it feels is that
the, certainly the skill...

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:29.280
- 10 is gonna be more interested

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:31.310
'cause I'll be in the eye of the storm.

00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:32.730
If you check it with me in five years

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:33.563
which I'm happy to do,

00:07:33.563 --> 00:07:35.520
I'm gonna be hyped 'cause
I'm like, "Here we go."

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:37.860
- There probably some
time in your schedule

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:40.800
just about freeing up a
9 or 10 years from now.

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:41.940
But you know, it's I do,

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:45.110
I spend as you do a ton
of time with young people,

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:46.540
you know, people who are
still in high school,

00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:47.650
people are still in college

00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:50.020
and I'm spending a bunch of time

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:51.610
with some TikTok people right now

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:54.550
who have this huge early fame.

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:58.660
And it's funny 'cause they
are very economically focused.

00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:01.153
I mean, to them, that's the hurdle.

00:08:02.771 --> 00:08:04.320
You try to say

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:07.020
and I never quite know
how it's getting through

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:11.150
that you will see that
there needs to be more to it

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:12.550
than that.

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:15.707
And that what I think of huge
advantage of when we hit 45

00:08:15.707 --> 00:08:19.110
and when I hit 45, as you
do have all these skills

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:23.770
and you go, "I know I
can do business stuff,

00:08:23.770 --> 00:08:26.740
but now I've gotta be selective
about what I want to apply

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:27.573
these skills to.

00:08:27.573 --> 00:08:29.090
What is the business that I want to do?

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:30.750
What is the non-business
that I want to do?

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:32.370
How do I want to use it?"

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:36.620
It's a really kinda interesting struggle.

00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:38.990
At least for me of saying...

00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:43.440
- I think when you're young
time is your teammate.

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:44.790
- Yes, exactly.

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:46.673
- And I think as you get older,

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:50.710
it's really how the brain works.

00:08:50.710 --> 00:08:53.160
When you're seven and you've gotta wait

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:54.020
to your 10th birthday,

00:08:54.020 --> 00:08:55.770
you believe you've been
on earth for seven years.

00:08:55.770 --> 00:08:57.300
That's half your life.

00:08:57.300 --> 00:08:58.530
Like half the life you lived

00:08:58.530 --> 00:09:01.320
is what you're anticipating
to be double digits.

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:02.830
But when you get to 45

00:09:02.830 --> 00:09:05.460
and one year is a small percent,
like it goes fast, right?

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:06.820
That's how you get...

00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:08.980
And so I think that's an
extremely valid point.

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:13.520
One of the things I worry about
is I live in such abundance

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:17.310
that I need to be thoughtful
about my time resource.

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:19.820
And I do feel like I'm
vulnerable to that mistake.

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:22.020
And it is something
that runs subconsciously

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:24.287
and is now become
conscious for me of like,

00:09:24.287 --> 00:09:27.880
"Hey, you value the
serendipity and the learning,

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:29.810
and the curiosity so much.

00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:31.050
You need to be careful here

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:33.840
because how many more
innings do you have?"

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:35.487
Plenty 'cause I'm a guy that's like,

00:09:35.487 --> 00:09:37.235
"I'm going for another 45,"

00:09:37.235 --> 00:09:40.320
but you still need to be thoughtful.

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:42.100
And you never know when a rock hits you

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:42.933
in the head and it's over.

00:09:42.933 --> 00:09:44.350
And so you gotta be thoughtful.

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:45.740
- It's also, I think that, you know,

00:09:45.740 --> 00:09:48.110
I think one of the things
that you and I have in common

00:09:48.110 --> 00:09:49.910
is also a huge curse,

00:09:49.910 --> 00:09:53.900
is that we see these huge opportunities.

00:09:53.900 --> 00:09:56.860
I mean, I know that
that's how you're wired.

00:09:56.860 --> 00:09:59.580
It's that almost life arbitrage thing.

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:01.160
And whether it's being at a garage sale

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:02.107
and seeing something I'll go,

00:10:02.107 --> 00:10:03.620
"Oh, my God, I can buy that for five bucks

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:04.780
and sell it for 20."

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:08.240
But I see that all the
time, not at garage sales,

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:10.880
but I see that in business opportunities.

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:13.140
Just like with Empathy,

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:15.670
where you go, "Oh my gosh,
there is this opening

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:17.870
for a native direct to consumer brand

00:10:17.870 --> 00:10:20.880
and you go, "Fuck, I'm
just gonna create it."

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:22.450
But the thing is those,

00:10:22.450 --> 00:10:25.050
especially as you get
better and get more attuned

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:28.690
and get better skills,
those things accelerate.

00:10:28.690 --> 00:10:30.030
And that's when you go, "Holy shit,

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:31.860
I've got to back up a little bit.

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:34.580
I've got to realize I've
got to focus a little bit

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:36.030
and kinda pick my battles."

00:10:36.030 --> 00:10:39.550
- I literary texted my brother
AJ today, four hours ago,

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:41.117
three hours ago,

00:10:41.117 --> 00:10:42.810
"Crippled by opportunity."

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:44.469
That was the text.

00:10:44.469 --> 00:10:47.410
- It's tricky. it really is tricky.

00:10:47.410 --> 00:10:49.073
And it's so hard.

00:10:50.545 --> 00:10:52.190
I personally feel it in all kinds of ways.

00:10:52.190 --> 00:10:53.560
I certainly feel it.

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:56.410
I'm so lucky now I get
all kinds of invitations

00:10:56.410 --> 00:10:59.570
either to invest or to advise
or to be part of things.

00:10:59.570 --> 00:11:02.563
And then it is such a struggle to say, no.

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:07.428
- Especially when you're an
optimist and an entrepreneur,

00:11:07.428 --> 00:11:09.623
you're in the yes business.

00:11:11.270 --> 00:11:12.650
- I once described it to someone

00:11:12.650 --> 00:11:15.290
as it's like someone who's
like walking by on the sidewalk

00:11:15.290 --> 00:11:17.440
and seeing a little basket full of puppies

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:18.830
with no one there.

00:11:18.830 --> 00:11:22.910
And God, you can't walk by
because someone's got to do this.

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:24.070
And whenever I see an opportunity,

00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:26.160
I go, "I just want to do it
and I want to make it real."

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:29.949
And no matter how
unrealistically impossible it is,

00:11:29.949 --> 00:11:32.680
it's a really tricky, fun.

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:35.120
I mean, I'm certainly glad
I was gifted with that.

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:37.210
It certainly has worked
out really well for me.

00:11:37.210 --> 00:11:39.900
But boy, sometimes I've
really got a struggle

00:11:40.879 --> 00:11:42.830
to control myself.

00:11:42.830 --> 00:11:43.663
- Yup.

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:47.610
- I got related or somewhat related ones.

00:11:47.610 --> 00:11:51.130
So since we brought up Empathy,
so probably you the same.

00:11:51.130 --> 00:11:52.620
One of the things I get asked all the time

00:11:52.620 --> 00:11:55.080
by entrepreneur is "When do I stop?

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:57.057
How do I know when to give up?"

00:11:58.810 --> 00:12:00.130
- I'll save your time.

00:12:00.130 --> 00:12:03.050
- I never know freaking
how to answer that.

00:12:03.050 --> 00:12:05.720
- It's impossibly difficult question.

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:07.723
The answer is nobody on earth knows.

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:13.740
- So I did a company after
Netflix which was Looker Data,

00:12:14.490 --> 00:12:16.490
which we tried to sell.

00:12:16.490 --> 00:12:18.223
And I look back,

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:21.617
it's such a 2020 hindsight thing

00:12:21.617 --> 00:12:23.820
and you did it 'cause of those information

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:24.730
you have at the time.

00:12:24.730 --> 00:12:27.070
And you look back and go,
"Ah, we should have wrote it.

00:12:27.070 --> 00:12:28.780
We were on the verge of going public.

00:12:28.780 --> 00:12:30.100
Just think what that would have been like

00:12:30.100 --> 00:12:32.520
not just as a business,
but as a cultural thing."

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:34.730
And I'm wondering with you
struggle with the same thing

00:12:34.730 --> 00:12:37.190
with Empathy, or even
before that with a Resy?

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:40.490
- No, Empathy and Resy were great exits.

00:12:40.490 --> 00:12:42.550
They're nine figure type deals.

00:12:42.550 --> 00:12:43.383
They were big wins.

00:12:43.383 --> 00:12:45.730
I had partners who were operating.

00:12:45.730 --> 00:12:49.200
I was more in chairman and founder level.

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:52.260
So I'd never really sold a company

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:54.320
that I'm navigating day to day.

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:58.560
They're both family businesses,
Vayner and Wine Library.

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:01.770
And so I didn't have any regrets

00:13:01.770 --> 00:13:04.740
with either because I wasn't the jockey.

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:08.480
I was the inventor of the
horse or a co-inventor,

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:09.683
but I wasn't the jockey.

00:13:10.618 --> 00:13:13.660
I might've been the trainer,
but I wasn't the jockey.

00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:18.660
And so I don't have those
feelings because of that scenario.

00:13:19.780 --> 00:13:21.020
And for everybody who's listening back

00:13:21.020 --> 00:13:22.533
to trying to provide value,

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:30.930
my dream of heaven is it'll
show you all the scenarios

00:13:30.930 --> 00:13:32.773
'cause I think that'd be really cool.

00:13:32.773 --> 00:13:34.460
But other than that,

00:13:34.460 --> 00:13:36.770
my make pretend of how
I want heaven to be.

00:13:36.770 --> 00:13:37.860
Nobody can ever tell you that

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:40.350
if you held onto it for another month

00:13:40.350 --> 00:13:43.970
that X would have
happened and that's cool.

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:46.100
And then there's a million
people who wrote it out

00:13:46.100 --> 00:13:47.410
for another three years and got a what?

00:13:47.410 --> 00:13:50.900
I mean, you and I have run in circles

00:13:50.900 --> 00:13:53.440
where we know incredible horror stories

00:13:53.440 --> 00:13:55.310
of people passing on selling their company

00:13:55.310 --> 00:13:57.040
for 210 million

00:13:57.040 --> 00:13:59.400
only to have it go out of business

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:02.290
or pass and selling it for 44 million

00:14:02.290 --> 00:14:04.650
only to have it sold for 19.

00:14:04.650 --> 00:14:09.140
Like people always think
of it as a one-way street.

00:14:09.140 --> 00:14:12.070
It goes both ways and that's important.

00:14:12.070 --> 00:14:12.980
- Oh, it certainly is.

00:14:12.980 --> 00:14:13.880
Especially, if you've been

00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:16.300
in certainly like I haven't
Silicon Valley for a long time,

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:18.240
you've seen so many horror stories.

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:20.060
It gets drilled into you

00:14:20.060 --> 00:14:22.230
that you don't want to bet your whole life

00:14:22.230 --> 00:14:24.050
on an uncertain future.

00:14:24.050 --> 00:14:25.910
I mean, there's one
thing to have confidence

00:14:25.910 --> 00:14:27.870
and it's one thing to take risks,

00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:29.610
but certainly, even
the biggest risk takers

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:31.410
do what they can to hedge the risks.

00:14:32.330 --> 00:14:35.550
One time, I worked for a
company called Borland,

00:14:35.550 --> 00:14:37.640
big software company a long time ago,

00:14:37.640 --> 00:14:39.980
was crushing it 'cause
we were looking like

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:41.550
we were gonna overtake Microsoft.

00:14:41.550 --> 00:14:43.870
If you can imagine how
ridiculous the assumption was

00:14:43.870 --> 00:14:45.040
back then.

00:14:45.040 --> 00:14:47.150
But I had stock options

00:14:47.150 --> 00:14:49.430
and they were worth a lot of money.

00:14:49.430 --> 00:14:53.900
And I was in a bar in Hong
Kong with our VP of sales

00:14:53.900 --> 00:14:57.990
on some sales tour, just to
put a crazy scenario here.

00:14:57.990 --> 00:15:01.870
And I was going, "God, this
stock price is going great."

00:15:01.870 --> 00:15:04.140
And he goes, "Yeah, I
hope you're selling."

00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:06.100
And I go, "Selling, why
would I want to sell?

00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:08.480
It's like, it's going up and up and up."

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:10.270
And he goes, "No, you
are out of your mind."

00:15:10.270 --> 00:15:14.310
He goes, "Every time
your stock vests, sell."

00:15:14.310 --> 00:15:17.930
And he goes, "You'll
always have more coming

00:15:17.930 --> 00:15:19.290
than you sold.

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:20.520
And if you're doing a great job,

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:22.720
they'll keep giving you more.

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:27.450
But if you don't sell anything,
your downside is unlimited.

00:15:27.450 --> 00:15:28.950
You could go to zero.

00:15:28.950 --> 00:15:32.550
All you're doing is taking
slight caps on your upside."

00:15:32.550 --> 00:15:37.300
And Ben, from that moment,
that was a long time ago.

00:15:37.300 --> 00:15:39.540
Every single chance I
get, I try and hedge.

00:15:39.540 --> 00:15:41.780
I mean, even with Looker
as that was going up.

00:15:41.780 --> 00:15:43.530
Every chance, every round,

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:45.380
we'd sell into the round a little bit.

00:15:45.380 --> 00:15:47.640
Just to take a little bit
of money off the table

00:15:47.640 --> 00:15:49.530
and go, "I have no idea
what's going to happen,

00:15:49.530 --> 00:15:52.379
but at least I have
something for my efforts."

00:15:52.379 --> 00:15:54.120
- I accept.

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:55.850
There's the other side of it.

00:15:55.850 --> 00:15:57.780
I was early into Facebook,

00:15:57.780 --> 00:16:01.340
but not as early as the
original 102 employees.

00:16:01.340 --> 00:16:03.730
A lot of those kids sold Facebook

00:16:04.686 --> 00:16:07.863
at 19 when it hit the floor at 25 and 27.

00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:10.323
That was a mistake.

00:16:11.680 --> 00:16:13.750
Some of those people were
thrilled to get the $10 million.

00:16:13.750 --> 00:16:17.870
The problem is 10 times
that it's a hundred.

00:16:17.870 --> 00:16:21.160
And so, you know, there's
a lot of ways to play it.

00:16:21.160 --> 00:16:25.150
- Yeah. I think the real key
is you just can't look back.

00:16:25.150 --> 00:16:26.440
You can't worry about this stuff.

00:16:26.440 --> 00:16:27.920
It's unknowable.

00:16:27.920 --> 00:16:29.637
You just got to move on and you go,

00:16:29.637 --> 00:16:31.990
"There'll be more fun yet to come."

00:16:31.990 --> 00:16:35.360
And I think the same people
who are always worried

00:16:35.360 --> 00:16:36.230
about what could have been,

00:16:36.230 --> 00:16:37.063
and I don't want to do this,

00:16:37.063 --> 00:16:38.220
and I make the wrong decision.

00:16:38.220 --> 00:16:40.370
The people who were
scared to move forward.

00:16:42.150 --> 00:16:44.510
Another great thing that you
and I kinda have in common

00:16:44.510 --> 00:16:48.670
is this whole most advancement
comes from just starting,

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:50.130
just doing something.

00:16:50.130 --> 00:16:52.053
Stop, stop thinking.

00:16:53.978 --> 00:16:55.960
I don't know how long ago
this was, you did that, what?

00:16:55.960 --> 00:16:58.130
7 or 800 page slide deck

00:16:58.130 --> 00:17:02.363
about how to do 60 some odd
pieces of social media in a day.

00:17:03.206 --> 00:17:05.160
And I remember it was one line in there,

00:17:05.160 --> 00:17:05.993
something that goes...

00:17:05.993 --> 00:17:08.410
"The thing that gets everyone in trouble

00:17:08.410 --> 00:17:12.090
is they have this standard
of production value."

00:17:12.090 --> 00:17:12.923
- Correct.

00:17:12.923 --> 00:17:15.430
- And so they just spend the
whole time tweaking fonts

00:17:15.430 --> 00:17:17.210
and rather than just
fucking putting it out there

00:17:17.210 --> 00:17:18.830
and then moving onto the next one.

00:17:18.830 --> 00:17:21.230
And that's so much more powerful.

00:17:21.230 --> 00:17:23.130
And God, that's something
that I've done with time

00:17:23.130 --> 00:17:25.940
and time and time again is trained myself

00:17:25.940 --> 00:17:27.990
that I'm totally okay shit.

00:17:27.990 --> 00:17:29.640
I'm totally okay.

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:31.740
Testing something that has broken links

00:17:31.740 --> 00:17:33.273
and it has bad images.

00:17:34.830 --> 00:17:37.360
You learn nothing from something perfect

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:38.393
that never sees the night and day.

00:17:38.393 --> 00:17:41.497
- I always say you can't
read about doing pushups.

00:17:41.497 --> 00:17:43.023
- (laughing) Yeah.

00:17:44.427 --> 00:17:45.260
I have a similar one.

00:17:45.260 --> 00:17:48.048
I go, "You can't learn to play
tennis by watching a video."

00:17:48.048 --> 00:17:49.681
- That's it.

00:17:49.681 --> 00:17:50.560
That's it.

00:17:50.560 --> 00:17:51.650
Exactly right.

00:17:51.650 --> 00:17:52.483
- Yeah. You got it.

00:17:52.483 --> 00:17:53.420
That was some over sharing.

00:17:53.420 --> 00:17:55.137
The other one is basically

00:17:55.137 --> 00:17:57.200
"The key to success is being willing

00:17:57.200 --> 00:17:59.896
to seem like you're a five-year-old."

00:17:59.896 --> 00:18:01.696
- You know, it's funny you say that.

00:18:02.570 --> 00:18:03.460
I'm writing a new book

00:18:03.460 --> 00:18:06.450
and that will be out
probably in the winter.

00:18:06.450 --> 00:18:09.920
And curiosity is a very big theme in it.

00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:10.753
- Yeah.

00:18:10.753 --> 00:18:13.610
- I really believe that that
has helped me quite a bit.

00:18:13.610 --> 00:18:16.670
- With curiosity, it's
not knowing in advance

00:18:16.670 --> 00:18:18.300
what can't be done

00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:20.927
and it's being willing
to look like an idiot.

00:18:20.927 --> 00:18:21.837
- Humility. Humility.

00:18:21.837 --> 00:18:24.450
- You speak any languages,
but you wanna learn French,

00:18:24.450 --> 00:18:25.890
You gotta sound like you're five years old

00:18:25.890 --> 00:18:26.723
for a couple of years.

00:18:26.723 --> 00:18:29.577
And being willing to go
into restaurants and say

00:18:29.577 --> 00:18:32.500
"I can't speak, but I'm gonna try."

00:18:32.500 --> 00:18:33.860
And that's how I'm gonna learn,

00:18:33.860 --> 00:18:34.880
- Humility.

00:18:34.880 --> 00:18:36.347
- Humility.

00:18:36.347 --> 00:18:38.143
- It's a big one too.

00:18:39.110 --> 00:18:39.943
It's funny.

00:18:39.943 --> 00:18:41.450
I'm very hot on those.

00:18:41.450 --> 00:18:42.670
Keep going.

00:18:42.670 --> 00:18:43.503
- No, it's cool.

00:18:43.503 --> 00:18:46.193
I mean, as you probably know,

00:18:46.193 --> 00:18:49.890
I'm trying to follow in the Gary V path.

00:18:49.890 --> 00:18:52.360
I have a podcast just when you're going.

00:18:52.360 --> 00:18:53.720
I'm not gonna interview for the podcast,

00:18:53.720 --> 00:18:56.000
but I'm going to do a kind of a podcast

00:18:56.000 --> 00:18:59.290
and mostly, not one where I interview

00:18:59.290 --> 00:19:01.580
really successful entrepreneurs
'cause that's been done,

00:19:01.580 --> 00:19:03.500
and done way better than I could do it.

00:19:03.500 --> 00:19:07.400
But kinda doing much more
along the ask Gary V stuff

00:19:07.400 --> 00:19:09.090
where I work with and mentor people,

00:19:09.090 --> 00:19:10.430
which I've been doing for 15 years

00:19:10.430 --> 00:19:11.910
and now I'm just recording it,

00:19:11.910 --> 00:19:13.410
but it's called "That'll Never Work".

00:19:13.410 --> 00:19:15.360
And one of the things
I was curious about...

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:17.840
One of my premises is
that every single person

00:19:17.840 --> 00:19:20.860
who does something new, here's that.

00:19:20.860 --> 00:19:22.657
You all come rushing
in and tell your wife,

00:19:22.657 --> 00:19:23.530
"That'll never work."

00:19:23.530 --> 00:19:25.480
You tell your investors,
"That'll never work."

00:19:25.480 --> 00:19:28.680
And I'm sure you've heard
that a hundred times.

00:19:28.680 --> 00:19:30.920
And I wonder if there's some
of those that really stand out

00:19:30.920 --> 00:19:33.060
in your mind when someone
told you that'll never work

00:19:33.060 --> 00:19:35.200
and what you were thinking at the time?

00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:37.840
- Well, this is a wild question from Marc

00:19:37.840 --> 00:19:40.430
because I would argue

00:19:40.430 --> 00:19:43.570
that my entire life

00:19:43.570 --> 00:19:44.737
has been that.

00:19:44.737 --> 00:19:47.010
(laughing)

00:19:47.010 --> 00:19:47.843
- I mean...

00:19:50.820 --> 00:19:53.000
I've been innovating my whole career.

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:54.740
You know, I was a bad student,

00:19:54.740 --> 00:19:58.330
so from day one, I was told
I would never be anything

00:19:58.330 --> 00:20:00.520
by the establishment outside of my mom.

00:20:00.520 --> 00:20:01.630
You know?

00:20:01.630 --> 00:20:04.600
So that was good 'cause
it made me not scared.

00:20:04.600 --> 00:20:05.433
- Right.

00:20:05.433 --> 00:20:07.660
- You know, like by the
time I became a grownup,

00:20:09.100 --> 00:20:10.540
I was told.

00:20:10.540 --> 00:20:12.480
But the one place I was never told that

00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:14.400
was that the consumer level.

00:20:14.400 --> 00:20:17.950
At the lemonade stand, at
the baseball card show,

00:20:17.950 --> 00:20:20.433
and my dad's liquor store, I was shining.

00:20:21.440 --> 00:20:24.040
The consumer always said yes to me,

00:20:24.040 --> 00:20:26.850
but humans who had judgment
of what I was up to

00:20:26.850 --> 00:20:28.320
always said, no.

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:29.730
I'm going through it right now.

00:20:29.730 --> 00:20:31.210
The last two weeks I've
been explaining to everybody

00:20:31.210 --> 00:20:33.260
what I'm gonna do in NFTs.

00:20:33.260 --> 00:20:34.590
Now I have some reputation,

00:20:34.590 --> 00:20:38.846
so people are a little bit more
thoughtful with their nose.

00:20:38.846 --> 00:20:41.680
- (laughing) They hedge their bets.

00:20:41.680 --> 00:20:44.230
- But I mean, my dad
was told by every liq...

00:20:44.230 --> 00:20:45.730
All these uncles of mine,

00:20:45.730 --> 00:20:48.080
other liquor store owners
that grew up knowing,

00:20:49.490 --> 00:20:51.270
they all told my dad he was crazy

00:20:51.270 --> 00:20:53.460
for letting me build a website.

00:20:53.460 --> 00:20:58.180
The internet was a fad, 1996, it's a fad.

00:20:58.180 --> 00:21:01.120
How can you spend $25,000, 15 on the side

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:02.880
and then a little to kick it off them?

00:21:02.880 --> 00:21:04.390
That was a lot of money
for a small business

00:21:04.390 --> 00:21:08.010
that does 300,000 in profit a year.

00:21:08.010 --> 00:21:12.170
And you know, building
winelibrary.com was crazy.

00:21:12.170 --> 00:21:15.620
Moving all the money from
print and direct mail

00:21:15.620 --> 00:21:17.393
to Google AdWords was crazy.

00:21:18.530 --> 00:21:21.780
Now I'd built this $40
million company from four

00:21:21.780 --> 00:21:23.450
and now I'm spending all my
time in this thing called

00:21:23.450 --> 00:21:24.810
YouTube and making videos.

00:21:24.810 --> 00:21:26.100
Who do I think I am?

00:21:26.100 --> 00:21:27.513
What is this? That's crazy.

00:21:28.418 --> 00:21:30.280
Then my financial
advisor said it was crazy

00:21:30.280 --> 00:21:31.770
that I put all my money that I'd saved

00:21:31.770 --> 00:21:33.430
into Facebook and Twitter stock.

00:21:33.430 --> 00:21:34.990
That was crazy.

00:21:34.990 --> 00:21:38.390
Starting an advertising agency
by being a wine merchant.

00:21:38.390 --> 00:21:40.160
That was crazy.

00:21:40.160 --> 00:21:42.973
You know, buying Bitcoin
in 2014 was crazy.

00:21:47.220 --> 00:21:49.190
I mean nothing, nothing...

00:21:49.190 --> 00:21:51.290
I don't really remember anything

00:21:51.290 --> 00:21:55.800
I was excited about that
wasn't met with blind.

00:21:55.800 --> 00:21:58.030
You can do it from my mom

00:21:58.030 --> 00:22:01.193
and then probably not from everybody else.

00:22:02.160 --> 00:22:03.773
That's a story of my life.

00:22:04.770 --> 00:22:07.150
- I've kind of learned
that my wife is almost

00:22:07.150 --> 00:22:09.170
a reverse indicator.

00:22:09.170 --> 00:22:12.140
That if she goes, "Uh, what?"

00:22:12.140 --> 00:22:14.080
I go, "I am onto something."

00:22:14.080 --> 00:22:15.400
- I love it.

00:22:15.400 --> 00:22:16.233
- Yeah, it's true.

00:22:16.233 --> 00:22:19.870
I mean, gosh, it's such
a huge piece of knowledge

00:22:19.870 --> 00:22:20.960
for anyone to gather,

00:22:20.960 --> 00:22:23.290
which is that no one else knows anything

00:22:23.290 --> 00:22:26.210
that they're all just
kinda using these patterns

00:22:26.210 --> 00:22:28.370
they've learned from other things

00:22:28.370 --> 00:22:29.940
and trying to handicap

00:22:29.940 --> 00:22:32.480
where there's something new
is actually gonna work or not.

00:22:32.480 --> 00:22:33.627
And so it's so much easier to go,

00:22:33.627 --> 00:22:35.300
"That'll never work because
they're probably gonna be

00:22:35.300 --> 00:22:36.470
right more times than not."

00:22:36.470 --> 00:22:41.470
But it's just such a terribly
de-motivating piece of advice.

00:22:41.560 --> 00:22:42.620
- It's happening to me right now.

00:22:42.620 --> 00:22:45.340
As I explained to people
that NFTs is gonna be

00:22:46.220 --> 00:22:48.470
the ultimate arbitrage for influencers

00:22:48.470 --> 00:22:50.163
that have build real communities.

00:22:51.394 --> 00:22:56.110
The way that artists are gonna
get their actual economics.

00:22:56.110 --> 00:22:59.510
The fact that people will
care about social currency

00:22:59.510 --> 00:23:02.540
at scale, virtual goods.

00:23:02.540 --> 00:23:05.050
You know, these are all
things that I believe in,

00:23:05.050 --> 00:23:06.370
and it's hard for people to understand.

00:23:06.370 --> 00:23:08.207
I have to say things to them like

00:23:08.207 --> 00:23:09.970
"How much do you think three years ago

